      <p class="byline">Lead-Acid Knowledge · Last reviewed 2026-09-16</p>
      <h1>Truck Battery: High CCA, High Capacity, and the N-Series</h1>
      <p class="lead">
        A truck (heavy-duty) battery is a large lead-acid battery built for <strong>very high cold cranking amps
        (CCA)</strong> to turn over big diesel engines, and high capacity (Ah) to run the vehicle's electrical load —
        all in a rugged, vibration-resistant case.
      </p>

      <div class="takeaways">
        <strong>Direct answer:</strong> Truck batteries come in large formats — the JIS <strong>N-series</strong>
        (N150, N200, N200L) and BCI <strong>Group 31, 4D and 8D</strong> — delivering hundreds to over a thousand
        cold cranking amps. Heavy trucks often use <strong>24V systems</strong> (two 12V batteries in series), so the
        battery spec and the system voltage are two separate things to get right.
      </div>

      <h2>Common truck battery formats</h2>
      <table>
        <thead><tr><th>Group / model</th><th>Typical capacity</th><th>Typical CCA</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><td><a href="https://data.worldbatteryhub.com/battery-groups/group31/">Group 31</a></td><td>~100 Ah</td><td>~900 A</td><td>Widespread North American heavy-duty</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="https://data.worldbatteryhub.com/battery-groups/n150/">N150</a> (145G51)</td><td>~135 Ah</td><td>~900 A</td><td>JIS heavy-duty</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="https://data.worldbatteryhub.com/battery-groups/n200/">N200</a> (190H52)</td><td>~200 Ah</td><td>~1100 A</td><td>JIS, very high capacity/CCA</td></tr>
          <tr><td><a href="https://data.worldbatteryhub.com/battery-groups/group8d/">8D</a></td><td>~200 Ah</td><td>~1400 A</td><td>Large BCI, heavy equipment</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
      <p class="muted">
        CCA is standard-dependent — a JIS value (tested at −15°C) reads higher than an EN/SAE value (tested at −18°C)
        for the same battery. Compare only within one standard. See
        <a href="/lead-acid/cca/what-is-cca/">what CCA is</a>.
      </p>
      <p>
        Read the CCA column with that standard caveat in mind: these are typical published values, and a JIS-rated
        N150 (~900 A at −15°C) is not the same test as a Group 31 rated under SAE at −18°C. The capacity column is
        the other half of the story — truck batteries run auxiliary loads (lift gates, sleeper cabs) with the engine
        off, which is why Ah matters as much as cranking here.
      </p>

      <h2>12V vs 24V: read the system, not just the battery</h2>
      <p>
        Light and medium trucks run on 12V. Many heavy trucks, buses and off-highway machines run on
        <strong>24V</strong>, built from two 12V batteries wired in series. The two batteries must be matched — same
        chemistry, capacity and age — or the pair ages unevenly and fails early.
      </p>
      <p>
        The matched-pair rule follows from series wiring: two batteries in series share the same current, so the
        weaker or older unit becomes the limiting one and the pair drags each other down. In practice fleets replace
        24V pairs as a matched set — same chemistry, capacity and age — because a fresh battery wired next to a tired
        one fails the pair early, not just itself.
      </p>

      <h2>What makes a truck battery different</h2>
      <ul>
        <li><strong>Thicker plates</strong> — for high capacity and durability under vibration and deep cycling.</li>
        <li><strong>High CCA</strong> — diesel engines compress harder and crank under more load, especially cold.</li>
        <li><strong>Rugged case and terminals</strong> — built for vibration, not just cranking.</li>
        <li><strong>Deep-cycle tolerance</strong> — truck batteries power hotel loads (lights, lift gates, sleeper cabs), not just starting.</li>
      </ul>

      <h2>Key takeaways</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <ul>
          <li>Truck batteries are large, high-CCA, high-Ah, vibration-resistant lead-acid batteries.</li>
          <li>Common formats: JIS N150/N200 and BCI Group 31/4D/8D.</li>
          <li>Heavy trucks often use 24V (two matched 12V in series) — never mix mismatched batteries.</li>
          <li>Always state the CCA standard when comparing truck batteries.</li>
        </ul>
      </div>
      <p>
        The one-line summary: a truck battery is the same lead-acid chemistry, scaled up and hardened — more lead for
        more Ah, more plate area for more CCA, and a case built for vibration. The detail that trips buyers is the
        rating standard, because the same battery prints very different CCA numbers across SAE, EN and JIS.
      </p>
      <h2>What buyers should ask</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>What group (N-series, Group 31/8D) and terminal type fit the truck?</li>
        <li>What CCA under SAE/EN does the spec require?</li>
        <li>Is the battery vibration-rated for its mounting location?</li>
      </ul>
      <h2>The heavy-duty numbers</h2>
      <p>
        The truck battery's design targets are written for the application: vibration resistance for chassis
        mounting, cycling capacity for sleeper-cab loads and lift-gate duty, and enough reserve for the auxiliary
        systems that run with the engine off. The same Ah label does different work here — which is why
        heavy-duty ratings carry vibration standards and reserve capacity numbers that light-duty batteries never
        list (see <a href="/lead-acid/reserve-capacity/">reserve capacity</a>).
      </p>



      <h2>What This Means</h2>
      <div class="takeaways">
        <p>
          <strong>Where I stand:</strong> The most expensive truck-battery mistake is not buying too small a battery — it is
          mismatching a 24V pair, which quietly destroys both batteries long before their rated life.
        </p>
        <p>
          <strong>The reasoning:</strong> Two mismatched 12V batteries in series do not "average out" — the weaker one is
          dragged down and the pair fails together. Matching chemistry, capacity and age is the whole ballgame in a
          24V truck system.
        </p>
        <p class="muted"><em>This is the author's editorial view, not a purchasing guarantee.</em></p>
      </div>

      <h2>Sources</h2>
      <ul>
        <li>Battery Council International (BCI) — Group 31/4D/8D size and rating references.</li>
        <li>JIS D5301 — N-series (N150/N200) capacity and CCA.</li>
        <li>Manufacturer datasheets (Varta, Exide, GS Yuasa, Dingwei/Chengguang) — truck battery CCA and capacity.</li>
      </ul>

      <p class="muted">Last reviewed: 2026-09-16</p>
    </div>
